# The Tale of the Two Weavers

In ancient times, two weavers lived on opposite shores of a silver river. The First Weaver possessed threads of brilliant color but had no loom. The Second Weaver owned a magnificent loom but had only gray thread.

One spring, the First Weaver called across the water: "I will send you my colored threads if you weave cloth and share it with me." The Second Weaver agreed, and the First Weaver placed crimson, gold, and azure threads in a boat and pushed it across.

The Second Weaver worked through the night, creating fabric that shimmered like sunset. But when morning came, greed whispered in their heart. "Why share this beauty?" they thought, and kept the cloth hidden.

Seasons passed. The First Weaver sent no more threads. The Second Weaver's loom fell silent, their fingers forgetting the patterns.

Years later, the Second Weaver crossed the river, carrying the faded cloth. "I betrayed your trust," they confessed. "I have come to return what I should have shared."

The First Weaver smiled sadly. "The threads were freely given. But trust, once broken, takes longer to weave than any tapestry." 

They began again, slowly, one thread at a time.
